{
  "dom_id": "step-q314062",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "${lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}{x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}}=${{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, we let\n\n$$\nL={lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}{x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}}.\n$$\n\nComparing this with the formula for the instantaneous rate of change\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)-f(a)}{x-a},\n$$\n\nwe see that if we let $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)$ and $a=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\frac{f(x)-f\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}{x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}}=L.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis confirms that $L={f}^{′}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$\n\nComputing the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\,{f}^{′}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\n$$\n\nand therefore, we conclude that $L=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
